p.src:
In the canonically normalized Standard Model convention, $H(x)=\frac1{\sqrt2}
  \begin{pmatrix}
    0\\ v+h(x)
  \end{pmatrix},
  \qquad v\simeq246\ \mathrm{GeV},$ and therefore $m_f=\frac{y_fv}{\sqrt2}.$ The lecture’s $f$ and the conventional $v/\sqrt2$ differ only by this choice of normalization.

